This package is derived from dsh-timer-agent, used under the MIT License. The
original project lives at:

    https://github.com/linxin666/dsh-timer-agent

Upstream ships real TypeScript sources, so this is a port rather than a
repackage: src/ is upstream's host-side code with the changes below, compiled
here with esbuild instead of tsdown.

Renamed throughout to @my-dsh/routines. The route family moved from
/api/dsh-timer-agent/* to /api/routines/*, the ledger from
~/.dsh/timer-agent/jobs.json to ~/.dsh/routines/jobs.json, and the
model-facing tool from `timer_agent` to `routines`. The route prefix is a
single exported constant, because a package name is not a URL path and the two
should not be able to drift into each other.

No sidebar. Upstream mounts a sidebar entry and a scheduled-jobs board; this
build carries neither, so the conversation surface is untouched. The whole of
upstream's src/client/ is dropped, and with it the CSS-module build step its
board needed. The browser half here is ours: one Routines section on the
settings page that lists routines and creates, pauses, resumes, runs, and
deletes them over the same routes.

English only. Upstream's dictionaries, sidebar label, tool description, and the
system-prompt guidance are Simplified Chinese; all of it is translated. The
guidance matters most, because it is the model's entire view of the capability.

What is kept from upstream, and is the reason to port rather than rewrite: the
engine. A 60-second ticker resident in the dsh web process, at-most-once firing
(nextRunAt rolls forward BEFORE a run, so a crash mid-run cannot double-fire),
skip-while-running, atomic ledger writes that degrade safely on a corrupt file,
5-field cron parsing with local-time semantics, and the three targeting modes
(project workdir / pinned session / default workspace).

Known limits, inherited and real: firing depends on the `dsh web` process being
alive, a slot missed while it was down stays missed rather than backfilling,
and every run costs API quota with nobody present to answer a question.

